WEBVTT

00:00:00.167 --> 00:00:02.867
You turned off every notification.

00:00:03.133 --> 00:00:05.267
No banners. No vibrations.

00:00:05.533 --> 00:00:08.433
No little red numbers asking to be opened.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.267
Your phone is silent,

00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:13.433
face down — exactly where you left it.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.833
And then, without hearing anything,

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:17.833
you reach for it anyway.

00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:21.700
That matters, because notifications and checking

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.600
are not the same problem.

In a randomized one-week study,

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.467
researchers found no significant reduction

00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:32.200
in how often people checked their phones —

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.900
or their total screen time — after turning notifications

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.200
off.

Silence removed the interruption.

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:43.100
It didn't close the exit.

So what does?

00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:46.567
First, turning off unnecessary notifications

00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:48.467
is not bad advice.

00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:52.367
A message can appear at exactly the wrong moment.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.533
Even if you do not open it,

00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:57.900
part of your attention has already been redirected.

00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.767
Now you know that something happened.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.467
Someone may be waiting.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.900
There may be new information behind the screen.

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:08.433
In one laboratory experiment,

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.367
receiving phone notifications

00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:14.100
disrupted performance on a sustained-attention task —

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:15.567
even when participants

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:20.033
did not interact with their phones.

So yes: notifications

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.000
can interrupt you.

But that only explains the moments

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.400
when your phone asks for attention.

00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:28.467
It does not explain the moments

00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:32.000
when your phone is silent and you check it anyway.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.700
And those moments are easy to miss

00:01:34.733 --> 00:01:37.467
because they do not feel like interruptions.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.433
There is no buzz to blame.

You simply find yourself

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.400
holding the phone.

Researchers tested what happened

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.467
when people disabled their smartphone notifications

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.700
for one week.

The study was preregistered,

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.367
included 205 participants,

00:01:54.367 --> 00:01:56.767
and used objective phone logs

00:01:56.767 --> 00:02:00.400
rather than relying only on estimates of screen time.

00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:04.800
The expected result seems obvious.

Fewer notifications

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.000
should mean fewer reasons to pick up the phone.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.400
But researchers found no significant reduction

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.433
in how often participants checked their phones.

00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:15.267
They found

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:18.867
no significant reduction in total screen time either.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.667
The intervention also did not produce

00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:24.900
clear improvements in reported productivity

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.267
or smartphone-related distraction.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.000
That does not mean nothing changed.

00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:32.367
Participants reported that their checking

00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:35.700
felt less habitual — more intentional —

00:02:35.733 --> 00:02:38.200
even though the objective number of checks

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.467
did not decline.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.000
They also reported more fear of missing out

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.400
during the notification-free week.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.067
The study does not prove that disabling notifications

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:52.400
is useless. It examined one intervention,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.567
over one week, in one group of people.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.167
But it challenges a simple story:

Notification appears.

00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:03.633
Person checks phone.

Remove notification.

00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:08.300
Checking disappears.

Real behavior was more complicated.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:12.467
Remove the notification, and the phone becomes quieter.

00:03:12.633 --> 00:03:13.500
But the person

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.500
can still wonder whether something has arrived.

00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:19.000
They can still unlock the screen

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.967
during a difficult paragraph.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.667
They can still reach for novelty

00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:26.233
when a task becomes briefly boring.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.133
They can still check without identifying

00:03:29.133 --> 00:03:31.433
any specific reason at all.

00:03:31.767 --> 00:03:35.433
Think of phone distraction as two different doors.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:38.000
The first is a prompted exit.

00:03:38.067 --> 00:03:41.200
Your phone makes a sound. The screen lights up.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.967
A badge appears. The device opens the door for you.

00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:47.667
Disabling unnecessary notifications

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:53.033
helps close this door.

The second is a self-created exit.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.100
Nothing happens on the phone.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.633
Instead, something happens inside the task.

00:03:58.733 --> 00:04:00.967
You reach a confusing sentence.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.967
You wait for a file to load.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.300
You're not sure what to do next.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.867
Or the work simply stops being

00:04:07.867 --> 00:04:11.567
interesting for five seconds.

The phone is nearby,

00:04:11.567 --> 00:04:13.767
familiar and easy to open.

00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:16.367
So you create the exit yourself.

00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:20.067
This distinction is an explanatory model,

00:04:20.067 --> 00:04:21.700
not a diagnosis.

00:04:21.867 --> 00:04:25.267
The notification study did not establish the precise

00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:27.367
reason behind every check.

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:30.100
And not every check is a problem.

00:04:30.133 --> 00:04:33.433
Phones are useful. Some messages are important.

00:04:33.533 --> 00:04:36.467
Sometimes checking the device is exactly

00:04:36.467 --> 00:04:38.067
what the task requires.

00:04:38.267 --> 00:04:41.467
The problem is not that the phone can be opened.

00:04:41.567 --> 00:04:44.300
It's when opening it becomes easier

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.600
than noticing why you're leaving.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.733
Another randomized trial tested

00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:50.867
a much stronger intervention.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.067
Instead of making the phone silent, participants

00:04:54.067 --> 00:04:57.167
blocked mobile internet access on their smartphones

00:04:57.167 --> 00:04:58.400
for two weeks.

00:04:58.567 --> 00:05:01.400
They could still receive calls and text messages.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.100
They could still use the internet on computers.

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:05.967
But the phone was no longer

00:05:05.967 --> 00:05:09.400
a constantly available portal to the online world.

00:05:09.533 --> 00:05:11.933
The intervention improved objectively

00:05:11.933 --> 00:05:13.900
measured sustained attention,

00:05:14.067 --> 00:05:17.233
along with self-reported well-being and mental health.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.000
But it was also difficult to follow.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.167
Many people who entered the study did not maintain

00:05:23.167 --> 00:05:26.567
the full intervention as required.

So the lesson

00:05:26.567 --> 00:05:29.067
is not that everyone should remove mobile

00:05:29.067 --> 00:05:33.167
internet for two weeks.

The more useful lesson is this:

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.500
A stronger intervention changed

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.267
what happened after someone reached for the phone.

00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:42.600
Silent mode removed a signal.

Blocking mobile internet

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.900
added friction between the impulse and the reward.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:47.100
That friction

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.067
can create a moment in which a different decision

00:05:50.067 --> 00:05:53.200
becomes possible.

For an ordinary work session,

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.567
we do not need to reproduce a two-week research

00:05:56.567 --> 00:05:59.467
intervention.

We need a smaller test.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:03.300
Choose one task and one 25-minute block.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:07.467
Not your entire morning.

Not a complete digital detox.

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:12.467
Just one task with a clear finish line.

Before you begin,

00:06:12.533 --> 00:06:15.500
decide what counts as necessary contact.

00:06:15.667 --> 00:06:17.967
If you are responsible for a child,

00:06:17.967 --> 00:06:21.400
waiting for an urgent call, managing a medical need

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.867
or using the phone for accessibility,

00:06:23.967 --> 00:06:25.833
keep that route available.

00:06:25.933 --> 00:06:29.700
The goal is not to make the phone dangerous or unusable.

00:06:29.767 --> 00:06:32.700
The goal is to separate necessary access

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.433
from automatic access.

00:06:34.767 --> 00:06:37.667
Then prepare the phone in four layers.

00:06:37.933 --> 00:06:41.100
Layer one: silence the prompted exits.

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:43.867
Disable non-essential notifications

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:47.200
for the duration of the block.

Keep only the channel

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:49.567
that genuinely needs to reach you.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:53.267
This closes the door your phone opens for you.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:58.367
Layer two: move it outside automatic reach.

Do not place

00:06:58.367 --> 00:07:01.800
the phone beside your hand with the screen facing down.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:04.100
That is still beside your hand.

00:07:04.133 --> 00:07:07.467
Place it somewhere that requires a visible action:

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:11.267
standing up, turning around or reaching into a bag.

00:07:11.267 --> 00:07:14.067
You are not trying to make checking impossible.

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.367
You are making it noticeable.

00:07:16.867 --> 00:07:19.667
Layer three: add digital friction.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:22.467
Temporarily disable mobile internet,

00:07:22.467 --> 00:07:26.333
activate a focus mode, or block only the applications

00:07:26.333 --> 00:07:28.433
that usually become exits.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:30.600
Choose the smallest restriction

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.833
that changes what happens when you unlock the phone.

00:07:33.933 --> 00:07:35.767
If you need an authenticator,

00:07:35.767 --> 00:07:38.767
calculator, timer or work application,

00:07:38.767 --> 00:07:40.367
preserve that function.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:42.800
The target is not the whole device.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:46.100
The target is the path you usually take without

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:49.867
deciding.

Layer four: schedule the next check.

00:07:50.167 --> 00:07:53.400
Write down when you are allowed to check again.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.333
Not “later.”

Write the actual time.

This matters

00:07:57.333 --> 00:08:01.067
because uncertainty creates its own reason to check.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:04.567
You are not promising to ignore the phone forever.

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:07.567
You are deciding when the door will open.

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.467
Now start the 25-minute block.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:13.667
Keep a small piece of paper beside the task.

00:08:13.867 --> 00:08:17.000
Every time you feel the impulse to reach for the phone,

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:20.467
make one mark.

Do not argue with the impulse.

00:08:20.467 --> 00:08:23.467
Do not diagnose it.

Do not congratulate yourself

00:08:23.533 --> 00:08:25.767
for resisting it.

Just mark it

00:08:25.767 --> 00:08:29.200
and return to the next visible action in the task.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:30.867
When the timer ends,

00:08:30.867 --> 00:08:33.300
check the phone if you still want to.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:35.000
Then count the marks.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.833
The result is not a score for discipline.

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:41.767
It's a map of the exits that were previously invisible.

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:45.600
If you made no marks, good.

Maybe notifications

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.033
were your main problem.

00:08:47.067 --> 00:08:49.367
Maybe this task held your attention.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:51.400
Or maybe the phone was not an

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:54.400
important exit during this particular session.

00:08:54.667 --> 00:08:57.867
If you made several marks, that's useful too.

00:08:57.900 --> 00:08:59.767
The phone may have been silent,

00:08:59.767 --> 00:09:02.433
but the checking path was still available.

00:09:02.567 --> 00:09:05.767
If you ignored the setup and opened an app anyway,

00:09:05.767 --> 00:09:08.000
do not restart the experiment to

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.433
protect a perfect result.

Record what happened.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:13.133
Which moment in the task

00:09:13.133 --> 00:09:15.467
came immediately before the check?

00:09:15.533 --> 00:09:17.767
Was the next action unclear?

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:21.300
Were you waiting?

Did the task become uncomfortable?

00:09:21.433 --> 00:09:24.767
Were you looking for information you genuinely needed?

00:09:24.933 --> 00:09:26.967
The experiment is not asking, “

00:09:26.967 --> 00:09:29.600
How addicted am I?”

It is asking:

00:09:29.667 --> 00:09:32.833
What made leaving easier than continuing?

00:09:32.933 --> 00:09:36.367
That question gives you something you can redesign.

00:09:36.667 --> 00:09:38.167
Turning off notifications

00:09:38.167 --> 00:09:42.067
is useful.

It closes the door your phone opens for you.

00:09:42.067 --> 00:09:45.233
But if you keep reaching for the device in silence,

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:48.033
there's another door — the one you open yourself.

00:09:48.667 --> 00:09:51.333
You do not need to throw away your phone.

00:09:51.333 --> 00:09:54.867
You do not need to transform your life for two weeks.

00:09:54.933 --> 00:09:57.300
For one 25-minute session,

00:09:57.333 --> 00:10:00.500
make checking require a decision again.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:04.000
Then notice the reach before you follow it.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.367
Because sometimes the most distracting notification

00:10:07.367 --> 00:10:09.600
is the one that never arrived.

00:10:09.733 --> 00:10:12.700
Try the Silent Door Test once.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:14.567
Count the reaches you notice

00:10:14.567 --> 00:10:17.067
before you count the minutes you save.
